--- !u!114 &367098896
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 367098895}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 375df1493f5f4ea4695f006ed81073cd, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  allPages:
  - pageNumber: 1
    pageTitle: Intro to Business Administration
    pageContent: "To be admitted from an Ontario high school into the UWindsor BComm
      program, you must have completed ENG4U (Grade 12 English) and at least one
      Grade 12 U-level Mathematics course\u200B. A minimum 70% grade in a 4U math
      is normally required, and the overall admission average (Top 6 Grade 12 U/M
      courses) should be at least 73% (approximately mid-70s)\u200B. The co-op stream
      is more competitive, typically requiring around a 78% average or higher for
      direct entry\u200B. It\u2019s recommended to take courses like Advanced Functions,
      Calculus and Vectors, or Data Management in high school to prepare for the
      quantitative aspects of the program. Strong English and communication skills
      are also important, as evidenced by the required ENG4U credit. (Learn more:
      Visit UWindsor\u2019s Admission Requirements for Business Administration.)"
    isCollected: 0
  - pageNumber: 2
    pageTitle: Year 1 (Business Fundamentals)
    pageContent: "In first year, BComm students build a broad foundation in business.
      You\u2019ll take introductory courses such as Introduction to Business, Microeconomics
      and Macroeconomics, Mathematics for Business, Business Communications, and
      an Introduction to Business Data Analysis (spreadsheets). By second semester,
      you\u2019ll also be introduced to Financial Accounting, Marketing, Human Resource
      Management, and Business Data Analysis (statistics). These courses give you
      a solid grounding in understanding how businesses operate, basic economic principles,
      quantitative skills, and effective communication in a business context."
    isCollected: 1
  - pageNumber: 3
    pageTitle: Year 2 (Core Business Knowledge)
    pageContent: "In second year, you dive deeper into core business disciplines.
      Courses include Managerial Accounting (using accounting data for decision-making),
      Organizational Behaviour (management and workplace dynamics), Business Finance
      I & II (financial management fundamentals), Management Information Systems
      (using IT in business), and Quantitative Decision Models (advanced business
      math for decision-making). You\u2019ll also study Business Ethics to understand
      ethical decision-making in management. Toward the end of second year, students
      choose an elective in an area of interest and may be introduced to topics like
      Business Law or Operations Management as options. By the end of Year 2, you
      will have completed most of the core curriculum that all business students
      share."
    isCollected: 1
  - pageNumber: 4
    pageTitle: Year 3 (Specialization and Advanced Topics)
    pageContent: "In third year, you begin to focus on your chosen specialization
      (major). While you continue with any remaining core courses, a significant
      portion of your classes will be in your concentration. For example, a Finance
      major will start taking courses like Investments or Financial Markets, whereas
      a Marketing major will delve into Consumer Behaviour and Marketing Strategy.
      You\u2019ll apply the broad knowledge from years 1 and 2 to more specific contexts
      in Accounting, Finance, Marketing, Human Resources, International Business,
      Business Analytics & Supply Chain, or Strategy & Entrepreneurship. This is
      also a year when many students participate in co-op work terms (if in co-op)
      or other experiential learning, applying classroom theories in real business
      settings. Networking with industry speakers and engaging in group projects
      become even more central in third year, linking theory to practice."
    isCollected: 1
  - pageNumber: 5
    pageTitle: Year 4 (Capstone and Specialization Mastery)
    pageContent: "In the final year, students take advanced courses in their specialization
      and round out any electives. A notable capstone course is Strategic Management
      \u2013 an integrative course where you analyze business cases and make strategic
      decisions, tying together knowledge from all areas of business. Specialization
      courses in fourth year are often high-level: for instance, Accounting students
      might take Advanced Accounting or Auditing, and Human Resources students might
      study Strategic HR Management. Many students also have a final co-op work term
      or major project in fourth year. By the end of Year 4, having completed about
      40 courses, you\u2019ll have both depth in your specialty and well-rounded
      business acumen. An honours thesis or major research project is an option for
      those who want an additional challenge or are considering graduate studies.
      Overall, each year of the program builds on the last, from fundamentals to
      specialization to strategic overview, ensuring graduates are career-ready and
      capable of big-picture thinking"
    isCollected: 1
  - pageNumber: 6
    pageTitle: Co-op and Experiential Learning
    pageContent: "UWindsor\u2019s Business Administration program strongly emphasizes
      learning by doing. The co-operative education (co-op) option allows you to
      integrate paid work experience into your BComm degree. Co-op students complete
      three four-month work terms (about 12 months total) in relevant business roles
      while still graduating in four years. A typical co-op schedule involves two
      years of classes, then alternating work and study terms: for example, you study
      in Fall and Winter of third year, work in the summer, work again in one term
      of fourth year, and finish your last academic term in the remaining semester.
      These placements are paid and provide invaluable real-world experience \u2013
      by the end, co-op grads have a full year of work experience on their resume."
    isCollected: 1
